            "reply_count": 1,
            "best_answer": true,
            "best_answer_content": "Hi @LH1\n\nhave you excluded some SW going to change buck4 voltage thru I2C ?\n\nIs eMMC IO supply on STM32MP135 at 3.3V too (check VDDSD1/VDDSD2 if relevant) ?\n\nCould you share the board model you are using. Is it a custom board where HW is not yet trustable ?\n\nBuck component choice and layout could be tricky.\n\nIs the 2.4V clean and stable or oscillating ?\n\nPlease look into\n\n[AN5474Getting started with STM32MP13x lines hardware development](https://www.st.com/resource/en/application_note/an5474-getting-started-with-stm32mp13x-lines-hardware-development-stmicroelectronics.pdf)\n\n[DS12792 Highly integrated power management IC for micro processor units](https://www.st.com/resource/en/datasheet/stpmic1.pdf)\n\n[AN5431 The STPMIC1 PCB layout guidlines](https://www.st.com/resource/en/application_note/an5431-the-stpmic1-pcb-layout-guidlines-stmicroelectronics.pdf)\n\nand maybe\n\n[AN5587 Integration of STM32MP13x MPU product lines and STPMIC1D / STPMIC1A on a wall adapter supply](https://www.st.com/resource/en/application_note/an5587-integration-of-stm32mp13x-mpu-product-lines-and-stpmic1d--stpmic1a-on-a-wall-adapter-supply-stmicroelectronics.pdf)\n\nRegards",
            "best_answer_author": "PatrickF",
            "best_answer_author_role": "ST Employee",
            "content_text": "The STM32MP135 is powered by PMIC1A. After initial power-up, the voltage at BUCK4 is normal at 3.3V, but during U-Boot flashing, the output voltage at VOUT4 drops to only 2.4V, causing the flashing to fail. The VDD and VDDQ pins of the eMMC are powered by the same power rail, VOUT4.",
            "images": [],
            "author": "LH1",
            "scraped_replies": [
